number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|Unconstrained delegation receives privileged hybrid-administrator logons|A controllable non-DC host or service permits unconstrained delegation AND approved telemetry establishes eligible privileged logons from identities administering hybrid assets.|Delegation flag; host control; eligible account restrictions; recent authorized logon telemetry; target privileges.|Remove unnecessary delegation and prevent privileged identities from authenticating to lower-trust services.|Allowed logon rights are not observed logons. Protected Users, nondelegable accounts, and missing telemetry limit the conclusion.|T1558;T1550.003|AC-6;IA-5|Protect delegated authentication material and separate privileged logon paths.||High
002|A controllable service can delegate to a hybrid bridge service|An unapproved principal controls a constrained-delegation identity AND its effective supported delegation targets include a service whose authorization allows control of a hybrid bridge.|Account control; delegation flags; exact target SPNs; protocol-transition constraints; target service authorization.|Restrict delegation targets and protect the delegating service identity.|An SPN pointing at a bridge host does not automatically grant host administration. Resolve service and identity authorization.|T1550.003|AC-3;AC-6;IA-5|Limit delegated authentication to the intended service and privilege.||High
003|Resource-based delegation can be changed on a hybrid bridge target|An unapproved principal can modify effective RBCD configuration for a bridge computer or service AND the supported authentication prerequisites and service authorization are satisfied.|Attribute-specific ACL; target RBCD descriptor; controllable service identity; domain behavior; target SPNs and rights.|Restrict RBCD configuration writes and use narrowly scoped service identities.|Do not assume any computer-object write is RBCD control or that a machine-account quota establishes a complete path.|T1098;T1550.003|AC-3;AC-6;CM-5|Protect delegation configuration that can change who authenticates to a control-plane service.||High
004|A roastable account operates the federation or synchronization service|A password-based service account with an SPN is bound to an active hybrid service AND its effective privileges reach that service's control plane.|Service binding; SPNs; encryption and account type; rotation policy; effective service/key privileges.|Use supported managed service identities and protect service-account password strength and privilege.|Roastability does not establish successful cracking. Do not collect tickets or password material.|T1558.003;T1556.007|IA-5;AC-6|Protect service authenticators supporting hybrid authentication.||High
005|Key-credential write authority reaches a hybrid administrator|An unapproved principal can make an effective supported key-credential modification to an AD identity AND PKINIT and current mapping behavior permit the resulting authentication AND the target administers a hybrid asset.|Attribute-specific effective access; object type; key trust prerequisites; DC certificate/PKINIT support; target authorization.|Restrict key-credential writes and review unexpected key metadata for privileged accounts.|Do not add keys or request authentication. GenericWrite and key-credential writes have object-specific constraints.|T1098;T1649|AC-3;IA-5;CM-5|Protect changes to alternative authenticators for privileged identities.||High
006|A nonapproved replication principal reaches synchronized privileged credentials|A nonapproved principal has the complete effective directory-replication permission set for a domain containing synchronized privileged identities.|Domain-root effective access; account status; replication scope; source-to-cloud mapping; privileged account inventory.|Remove unintended replication rights and separate privileged cloud identities from AD password authority.|Do not retrieve secrets. Replicated AD material does not directly equal a reusable Entra password hash or bypass MFA.|T1003.006;T1078.004|AC-6;IA-5|Limit secret-replication authority and reduce the downstream privilege exposed by it.||High
007|AD database backup access reaches the hybrid administrative boundary|An unapproved principal can access an authorized backup containing AD credential material AND the affected domain includes identities authorized to administer hybrid assets.|Backup content classification; effective read/decrypt authorization; source domain; privileged host-access relationships.|Protect backup read and decryption rights to the control-plane boundary.|Do not open NTDS or credential stores. Backup file access without decryption ability is not established credential usability.|T1003.003;T1558.001|AC-6;CP-9;IA-5|Protect backup copies of authenticators that underpin hybrid administration.||High
008|A nonapproved RODC caching policy includes hybrid administrators|A lower-trust read-only domain controller is allowed to cache credentials for identities that administer hybrid assets AND current resultant password-replication policy permits those identities.|RODC role/location classification; resultant allow/deny policy; protected-group expansion; target hybrid privileges; cached-state metadata where authorized.|Deny credential caching for control-plane identities and review the RODC boundary.|Allowed caching is not proof credentials were cached. An effective deny overrides a broad allow.|T1003;T1078.002|AC-6;IA-5|Limit where privileged authentication material may be retained.||High
